# OpenClaw — Agent Robustness Benchmarks

## Purpose

Show operators how well each candidate LLM holds up against a fixed battery
of stress tests: baseline correctness, concurrency, adversarial prompts,
transient faults, and rate-limit behaviour. The data comes from
`mcp-universe-benchmarks` and is baked into the repo as a JSONL corpus.

This is a **read-only observability domain**. It does not launch new stress
runs from the UI. To regenerate the corpus, re-run the stress harness
externally and update the checked-in JSONL — see
`corpus/stress-benchmarks/PROVENANCE.md`.

## Boundary with the other engines

| Concern | Owner | Path |
|---|---|---|
| Live L1-L4 skill judgment (Archon) | **in-process** | `artifacts/api-server/src/lib/archon/*` |
| Optional external judge | reachability-probed | `artifacts/api-server/src/lib/benchmarkClient.ts` |
| Stress-test observability | **this domain** | `artifacts/api-server/src/lib/stress-benchmarks/*` |

If you find yourself extending stress-benchmarks to *run* new evaluations,
stop — that belongs in the Archon module or in `mcp-universe-benchmarks`
itself, not here.

## Backend — `artifacts/api-server/`

```
src/
├── lib/stress-benchmarks/
│   ├── types.ts        # StressRun, StressSummary, LeaderboardEntry, etc.
│   ├── runStore.ts     # JSONL load + cache + health probe
│   ├── aggregate.ts    # leaderboard, breakdowns, filtered pagination, facets
│   └── README.md
├── routes/
│   └── stressBenchmarks.ts  # mounted at /api/stress-benchmarks/*
└── corpus/stress-benchmarks/
    ├── runs.jsonl              # canonical corpus
    ├── stress_summary.json     # precomputed rollup (fallback)
    └── PROVENANCE.md
```

### Endpoints

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/health` | corpus availability, row count |
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/summary` | n_runs, provenance, leaderboard, all breakdowns |
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/leaderboard` | leaderboard only |
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/models` | distinct model IDs |
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/categories` | per-category rollup + domains + failure classes |
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/facets` | distinct models × categories × domains × perturbations |
| GET | `/api/stress-benchmarks/runs?model=&category=&domain=&perturbation_id=&passed=&limit=&offset=` | filtered + paginated raw rows |

Registered in `src/routes/index.ts` before `statusRouter`. The `/api/status`
endpoint reports a `stress_benchmarks` check (see `routes/status.ts`).

### Corpus contract

`runs.jsonl` is the source of truth. One row = one (model × task ×
perturbation × run_id) evaluation. The full row schema is versioned in
`lib/stress-benchmarks/types.ts` and mirrored in
`corpus/stress-benchmarks/PROVENANCE.md`. Changing the schema requires
updating both.

Sizing guidance: the in-memory cache is fine up to ~50 MB / ~500k rows. If
the corpus grows past that, migrate to SQLite/DuckDB — the aggregation
functions are pure and easy to port.

## Frontend — `artifacts/openclaw-saas/`

```
src/pages/agent-robustness/
├── index.tsx    # page shell + tabs
└── hooks.ts     # TanStack Query bindings for /api/stress-benchmarks/*
```

- Route registered in `src/App.tsx` at `/agent-robustness` (behind
  `ProtectedRoute`).
- Nav entry added to `src/components/Layout.tsx` (Activity icon).
- Tab layout: **Overview** + 5 category sub-tabs (baseline, concurrency,
  adversarial, faults, ratelimit).
- Uses existing `@/components/ui/{tabs,table,card,badge,skeleton,progress}`
  primitives and `@/lib/apiFetch` for cross-origin calls.

## Environment

- `STRESS_CORPUS_DIR` — optional override for corpus location. Default is
  `<api-server-cwd>/corpus/stress-benchmarks`.
- No new secrets. No external service calls. No new DB tables.

## What NOT to do here

- **Do not** re-introduce a live benchmark runner in this module. Use
  `lib/archon/benchmarkRunner.ts` for that.
- **Do not** couple to Clerk auth in the route file — the endpoints are
  read-only and safe to expose behind the shared middleware.
- **Do not** copy the corpus into a database at boot. The in-memory cache
  is intentional; migrating to SQLite is a follow-up when the size
  demands it.
- **Do not** add mutation endpoints (POST/PUT/DELETE). If a user wants to
  add data, they update the JSONL and redeploy.
